 <similarity> score=1.0719 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0169 code_feature=0.5714 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/320afe60.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/320afe60.json </paths> <description> Slide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.  Input structure: - Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color. - The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects. - Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.  Rule: - If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green. - Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red. - Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.  Key insight: - The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_along_path",   "src": "4-connected blue foreground objects",   "path": "horizontal slide in the same rows to the selected grid edge",   "dst": "left edge for closed or solid objects; right edge for objects with a flanked open notch on the bbox border",   "trajectory": "translate the whole object without changing its shape",   "transform": "recolor left-moving objects red and right-moving objects green" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="2546ccf6"> <similarity> score=1.0702 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0083 code_feature=0.5714 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/2546ccf6.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2546ccf6.json </paths> <description> Complete each 2-by-2 lattice-cell quartet by adding its missing reflected sprite.  Input structure: - The background is the most common color. - One non-background color forms complete horizontal and vertical divider   lines, partitioning the grid into rectangular cells. - Every occupied cell away from the dividers contains pixels of exactly one   non-background, non-divider color. - For each sprite color, occupied cells occur as connected three-cell groups   inside a 2-by-2 block of equal-sized cells.  Rule: - Split the grid at the divider rows and columns. - For each three-cell monochrome group in a 2-by-2 cell block, find the one   empty cell in that block. - Reflect each of the three present cell-local sprites vertically and/or   horizontally according to its relative position to the missing cell; all   three reflections must produce the same mask. - Paint that agreed reflected sprite into the missing cell, leaving dividers   and all existing cells unchanged.  Key insight: - The sprites are mirror variants around the center of a 2-by-2 block of   lattice cells, so any three visible variants uniquely determine the fourth. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "symmetry/pattern",   "src": "monochrome sprites occupying three cells of a 2x2 block in the divider-line lattice",   "dst": "empty fourth cell of each same-color 2x2 block",   "transform": "reflect the present cell-local sprite masks horizontally and/or vertically around the 2x2 block center; paint the unique agreed reflected sprite into the empty cell and preserve the lattice" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="5" source="train" puzzle_id="52364a65"> <similarity> score=1.0609 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0047 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/52364a65.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/52364a65.json </paths> <description> Erase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.  Input structure: - A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects. - Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.  Rule: - Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object. - Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color. - Leave all other pixels unchanged.  Key insight: - The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects on a background",   "transform": "for each object, set foreground pixels in its two leftmost bbox columns to bg" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="6" source="train" puzzle_id="df978a02"> <similarity> score=1.0712 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0131 code_feature=0.5714 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/df978a02.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/df978a02.json </paths> <description> Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.  Input structure - The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects. - The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions. - Exactly one object has the largest area.  Rule - Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects. - Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background. - Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.  Key insight - The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "four 8-connected monochromatic objects pointing toward a shared central bg pixel",   "transform": "identify the unique largest object; recolor each other object's nearest-to-center tip pixel to bg and add a 3-pixel cap in the largest object's color on its side away from the center" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="f931b4a8"> <similarity> score=1.0540 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0130 code_feature=0.4286 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/f931b4a8.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f931b4a8.json </paths> <description> Count the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.  Input structure: - The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants. - Black is the background. - The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions. - The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.  Rule: - Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant. - Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant. - If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position. - If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.  Key insight: - The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "symmetry/pattern",   "src": "lower-left color source tile and lower-right stencil tile",   "dst": "output rectangle sized by foreground counts in the upper-left and upper-right control quadrants",   "transform": "tile the lower-right stencil; replace each stencil background pixel with the corresponding coarse lower-left color, or tile the lower-left region directly when the stencil is empty",   "grid_shape": "height = count(upper-left foregro