or and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects. - Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.  Rule: - Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object. - Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color. - Leave all other pixels unchanged.  Key insight: - The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects on a background",   "transform": "for each object, set foreground pixels in its two leftmost bbox columns to bg" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="6" source="train" puzzle_id="df978a02"> <similarity> score=1.0712 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0131 code_feature=0.5714 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/df978a02.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/df978a02.json </paths> <description> Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.  Input structure - The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects. - The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions. - Exactly one object has the largest area.  Rule - Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects. - Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background. - Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.  Key insight - The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "four 8-connected monochromatic objects pointing toward a shared central bg pixel",   "transform": "identify the unique largest object; recolor each other object's nearest-to-center tip pixel to bg and add a 3-pixel cap in the largest object's color on its side away from the center" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="f931b4a8"> <similarity> score=1.0540 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0130 code_feature=0.4286 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/f931b4a8.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f931b4a8.json </paths> <description> Count the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.  Input structure: - The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants. - Black is the background. - The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions. - The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.  Rule: - Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant. - Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant. - If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position. - If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.  Key insight: - The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "symmetry/pattern",   "src": "lower-left color source tile and lower-right stencil tile",   "dst": "output rectangle sized by foreground counts in the upper-left and upper-right control quadrants",   "transform": "tile the lower-right stencil; replace each stencil background pixel with the corresponding coarse lower-left color, or tile the lower-lef